1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,300
Your phone buzzes on payday morning: 15 million dong credited to your checking account.

2
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:05,850
Yet between bank vaults, not one dong of cash actually moved across town.

3
00:00:06,580 --> 00:00:13,580
Because your employer and you hold accounts at the exact same bank, cash never leaves the building.

4
00:00:13,580 --> 00:00:20,290
The bank simply edits two lines inside one ledger: less owed to your company, more owed to you.

5
00:00:21,130 --> 00:00:28,140
Unspent pay sitting idle in checking is called CASA, paying roughly 0.1% yearly interest in this example.

6
00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:35,090
Across 500 coworkers leaving 5 million dong each, the bank builds an instant 2.5 billion dong cash pool.

7
00:00:35,650 --> 00:00:45,130
On that entire 2.5 billion dong pool, the bank pays only 2.5 million dong a year in interest.

8
00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:54,410
Raising the same cash with 5% term deposits would cost 125 million dong — fifty times more.

9
00:00:55,170 --> 00:01:01,450
In exchange for steering all those monthly paychecks into one house, your employer often wins lower wire fees or cheaper corporate borrowing rates.

10
00:01:02,510 --> 00:01:11,730
So tonight your salary balance sleeps inside the bank's general ledger — as an obligation the bank owes you while putting your idle cash to work.
